window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2020-12-3", "source": "2020年12月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part , you are allowed 30 minutes to write on the topic Changes in the Way of Communication. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "The way people communicate has changed dramatically over the past few decades. In the past, staying in touch\nmeant writing letters or making phone calls, while nowadays a tap on the screen connects us instantly. In my\nview, this shift has reshaped our lives in profound ways.\nThe most obvious change lies in speed and distance. Our parents used to wait days for a letter to arrive,\nwhereas we now send messages, photos and even video calls across continents in seconds. Take my\ngrandmother as an example: she once relied on the post office, but today she chats with me through instant\nmessaging and social media every night. Such tools help families stay connected regardless of where they live.\nHowever, this convenience comes at a price. When face-to-face talk is replaced by screens, warmth and\nsincerity may fade away. Therefore, we should embrace new means of communication while still valuing\ngenuine human contact. Only by balancing the two can we truly benefit from these changes."}, "translation": {"prompt": "鱼是春节前夕餐桌上不可或缺的一道菜，因为汉语中\"鱼\"字的发音与\"余\"字的发音相同。正由于这个象征性的意义，春节期间鱼也作为礼物送给亲戚朋友。鱼的象征意义据说源于中国传统文化。中国人有节省的传统，他们认为节省得愈多，就感到愈为安全。今天，尽管人们愈来愈富裕了，但他们仍然认为节省是一种值得弘扬的美德。", "sample": "Fish is an indispensable dish on the dinner table on the Spring Festival Eve, because the Chinese character ⻥ (fish) has the same pronunciation as 余 (surplus). Because of this symbolic meaning, fish is also given as a gift to relatives and friends during the Spring Festival. The symbolic meaning of fish is said to originate from traditional Chinese culture. The Chinese have a tradition of being thrifty, and they believe that the more they save, the more secure they feel. Today, although people are getting richer and richer, they still believe that thrift is a virtue worth promoting."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "The things people make, and the way they make them, determine how cities grow and decline, and influence how empires rise and fall. So, any disruption to the world's factories  __26__  . And that disruption is surely coming. Factories are being digitised, filled with new sensors and new computers to make them quicker, more  __27__  , and more efficient.\nRobots are breaking free from the cages that surround them, learning new skills and new ways of working. And 3D printers have long  __28__  a world where you can make anything, anywhere, from a computerised design. That vision is  __29__  closer to reality. These forces will lead to cleaner factories, producing better goods at lower prices, personalised to our individual needs and desires. Humans will be  __30__  many of the dirty, repetitive, and dangerous jobs that have long been a  __31__  of factory life.\nGreater efficiency  __32__  means fewer people can do the same work. Yet factory bosses in many developed countries are worried about a lack of skilled human workers—and see  __33__  and robots as a solution. But economist Helena Leurent says this period of rapid change in manufacturing is a  __34__  opportunity to make the world a better place. \"Manufacturing is the one system where you have got the biggest source of innovation, the biggest source of economic growth, and the biggest source of great jobs in the past. You can see it changing. That's an opportunity to  __35__  that system differently, and if we can, it will have tremendous significance.\"", "matchIntro": "The History of the Lunch Box", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "It was made of shiny, bright pink plastic with a Little Mermaid sticker on the front, and I carried it with me nearly every single day. My lunch box was one of my first prized possessions, a proud statement to everyone in my kindergarten: \"I love Mermaid-Ariel on my lunch box.\""}, {"label": "B", "text": "That bulky container served me well through my first and second grades, until the live-action version of 101 Dalmatians hit theaters, and I needed the newest red plastic box with characters like Pongo and Perdita on the front. I know I'm not alone here—I bet you loved your first lunchbox, too."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Lunch boxes have been connecting kids to cartoons and TV shows and super-heroes for decades. But it wasn't always that way. Once upon a time, they weren't even boxes. As schools have changed in the past century, the midday meal container has evolved right along with them."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Let's start back at the beginning of the 20th century—the beginning of the lunch box story, really. While there were neighborhood schools in cities and suburbs, one-room schoolhouses were common in rural areas. As grandparents have been saying for generations, kids would travel miles to school in the countryside (often on foot)."}, {"label": "E", "text": "\"You had kids in rural areas who couldn't go home from school for lunch, so bringing your lunch wrapped in a cloth, in oiled paper, in a little wooden box or something like that was a very long-standing rural tradition,\" says Paula Johnson, head of food history section at the Smithsonian National Museum of American History in Washington, D.C."}, {"label": "F", "text": "City kids, on the other hand, went home for lunch and came back. Since they rarely carried a meal, the few metal lunch buckets on the market were mainly for tradesmen and factory workers."}, {"label": "G", "text": "After World War II, a bunch of changes reshaped schools—and lunches. More women joined the workforce. Small schools consolidated into larger ones, meaning more students were farther away from home. And the National School Lunch Act in 1946 made cafeterias much more common. Still, there wasn't much of a market for lunch containers—yet. Students who carried their lunch often did so in a re-purposed bucket or tin of some kind."}, {"label": "H", "text": "And then everything changed in the year of 1950. You might as well call it the Year of the Lunch Box, thanks in large part to a genius move by a Nashville-based manufacturer, Aladdin Industries. The company already made square metal meal containers, the kind workers carried, and some had started to show up in the hands of school kids."}, {"label": "I", "text": "But these containers were really durable, lasting years on end. That was great for the consumer, not so much for the manufacturer. So executives at Aladdin hit on an idea that would harness the newfound popularity of television. They covered lunch boxes with striking red paint and added a picture of TV and radio cowboy Hopalong Cassidy on the front."}, {"label": "J", "text": "The company sold 600,000 units the first year. It was a major \"Ah-ha!\" moment, and a wave of other manufacturers jumped on board to capitalize on new TV shows and movies. \"The Partridge Family, the Addams Family, the Six Million Dollar Man, the Bionic Woman—everything that was on television ended up on a lunch box,\" says Allen Woodall. He's the founder of the Lunch Box Museum in Columbus, Georgia. \"It was a great marketing tool because kids were taking that TV show to school with them, and then when they got home they had them captured back on TV,\" he says."}, {"label": "K", "text": "And yes, you read that right: There is a lunch box museum, right near the Chattahoochee River. Woodall has more than 2,000 items on display. His favorite? The Green Hornet lunch box, because he used to listen to the radio show back in the 1940s."}, {"label": "L", "text": "The new trend was also a great example of planned obsolescence, that is, to design a product so that it will soon become unfashionable or impossible to use and will need replacing. Kids would beg for a new lunch box every year to keep up with the newest characters, even if their old lunchbox was perfectly usable."}, {"label": "M", "text": "The metal lunch box craze lasted until the mid-1980s, when plastic took over. Two theories exist as to why. The first—and most likely—is that plastic had simply become cheaper. The second theory—possibly an urban myth—is that concerned parents in several states proposed bans on metal lunch boxes, claiming kids were using them as \"weapons\" to hit one another. There's a lot on the internet about a state-wide ban in Florida, but a few days worth of digging by a historian at the Florida State Historical Society found no such legislation. Either way, the metal lunch box was out."}, {"label": "N", "text": "The last few decades have brought a new lunch box revolution, of sorts. Plastic boxes changed to lined cloth sacks, and eventually, globalism brought tiffin containers from India and bento boxes from Japan. Even the old metal lunch boxes have regained popularity. \"I don't think the heyday(鼎盛时期) has passed,\" says D.J. Jayasekara, owner and founder of lunchbox.com, a retailer in Pasadena, California. \"I think it has evolved. The days of the ready-made, 'you stick it in a lunchbox and carry it to school' are kind of done.\""}, {"label": "O", "text": "The introduction of backpacks changed the lunch box scene a bit, he adds. Once kids started carrying book bags, that bulky traditional lunch box was hard to fit inside. \"But you can't just throw a sandwich in a backpack,\" Jayasekara says. \"It still has to go into a container.\" That is, in part, why smaller and softer containers have taken off—they fit into backpacks."}, {"label": "P", "text": "And don't worry—whether it's a plastic bento box or a cloth bag, lunch containers can still easily be covered with popular culture. \"We keep pace with the movie industries so we can predict which characters are going to be popular for the coming months,\" Jayasekara says. \"You know, kids are kids.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "A growing number of U.S. bike riders are attracted to electric bikes for convenience, health benefits and their fun factor. Although ebikes first appeared in the 90s, cheaper options and longer-lasting batteries are breathing new life into the concept.\nEstablished bike companies and startups are embracing ebikes to meet demand. About 34 million ebikes were sold worldwide last year, according to data from eCycleElectric Consultants. Most were sold in Europe and China, where the bikes already have exploded in popularity. Recently, the U.S. market has grown to 263,000 bikes, a 25% gain from the prior year.\nThe industry is benefiting from improved batteries as suppliers over the years developed technology for laptops, smartphones and electric cars. In 2004, the price of batteries used on ebikes fell, spurring European sales.\nBut lower cost options are emerging, too. This month, three U.S. bikeshare companies, Motivate, LimeBike and Spin, announced electric bicycles will be added to their fleets. New York-based Jump Bikes is already operating an electric bikeshare in Washington, D.C., and is launching in San Francisco Thursday. Rides cost $2 for 30 minutes.\nThe system works like existing dockless bikeshare systems, where riders unlock bikes through a smartphone app. \"This is the beginning of a long-term shift away from regular pedal(踏板) to electric bikes,\" said Jump Bikes CEO Ryan Rzepecki. \"When people first jump on an ebike, their face lights up. It's exciting and joyful in a way that you don't get from a regular bike.\"\nTwo years ago, CEO Chris Cocalis of Pivot Cycles, which sells high-end mountain bikes, found that U.S. bike shops weren't interested in stocking ebikes. Some retailers warned Cocalis that they'd drop the brand if it came out with an electric bike.\nNow that sales are taking off, the vast majority of bike dealers are asking Cocalis when he'll make an ebike available. \"There's tremendous opportunity to get a generation of people for whom suffering isn't their thing,\" Cocalis said. \"Ebike riders get the enjoyable part of cycling without the massive suffering of climbing huge hills.\"", "47": "A growing number of U.S. bike riders are attracted to electric bikes for convenience, health benefits and their fun factor. Although ebikes first appeared in the 90s, cheaper options and longer-lasting batteries are breathing new life into the concept.\nEstablished bike companies and startups are embracing ebikes to meet demand. About 34 million ebikes were sold worldwide last year, according to data from eCycleElectric Consultants. Most were sold in Europe and China, where the bikes already have exploded in popularity. Recently, the U.S. market has grown to 263,000 bikes, a 25% gain from the prior year.\nThe industry is benefiting from improved batteries as suppliers over the years developed technology for laptops, smartphones and electric cars. In 2004, the price of batteries used on ebikes fell, spurring European sales.\nBut lower cost options are emerging, too. This month, three U.S. bikeshare companies, Motivate, LimeBike and Spin, announced electric bicycles will be added to their fleets. New York-based Jump Bikes is already operating an electric bikeshare in Washington, D.C., and is launching in San Francisco Thursday. Rides cost $2 for 30 minutes.\nThe system works like existing dockless bikeshare systems, where riders unlock bikes through a smartphone app. \"This is the beginning of a long-term shift away from regular pedal(踏板) to electric bikes,\" said Jump Bikes CEO Ryan Rzepecki. \"When people first jump on an ebike, their face lights up. It's exciting and joyful in a way that you don't get from a regular bike.\"\nTwo years ago, CEO Chris Cocalis of Pivot Cycles, which sells high-end mountain bikes, found that U.S. bike shops weren't interested in stocking ebikes. Some retailers warned Cocalis that they'd drop the brand if it came out with an electric bike.\nNow that sales are taking off, the vast majority of bike dealers are asking Cocalis when he'll make an ebike available. \"There's tremendous opportunity to get a generation of people for whom suffering isn't their thing,\" Cocalis said. \"Ebike riders get the enjoyable part of cycling without the massive suffering of climbing huge hills.\"", "48": "A growing number of U.S. bike riders are attracted to electric bikes for convenience, health benefits and their fun factor. Although ebikes first appeared in the 90s, cheaper options and longer-lasting batteries are breathing new life into the concept.\nEstablished bike companies and startups are embracing ebikes to meet demand. About 34 million ebikes were sold worldwide last year, according to data from eCycleElectric Consultants. Most were sold in Europe and China, where the bikes already have exploded in popularity. Recently, the U.S. market has grown to 263,000 bikes, a 25% gain from the prior year.\nThe industry is benefiting from improved batteries as suppliers over the years developed technology for laptops, smartphones and electric cars. In 2004, the price of batteries used on ebikes fell, spurring European sales.\nBut lower cost options are emerging, too. This month, three U.S. bikeshare companies, Motivate, LimeBike and Spin, announced electric bicycles will be added to their fleets. New York-based Jump Bikes is already operating an electric bikeshare in Washington, D.C., and is launching in San Francisco Thursday. Rides cost $2 for 30 minutes.\nThe system works like existing dockless bikeshare systems, where riders unlock bikes through a smartphone app. \"This is the beginning of a long-term shift away from regular pedal(踏板) to electric bikes,\" said Jump Bikes CEO Ryan Rzepecki. \"When people first jump on an ebike, their face lights up. It's exciting and joyful in a way that you don't get from a regular bike.\"\nTwo years ago, CEO Chris Cocalis of Pivot Cycles, which sells high-end mountain bikes, found that U.S. bike shops weren't interested in stocking ebikes. Some retailers warned Cocalis that they'd drop the brand if it came out with an electric bike.\nNow that sales are taking off, the vast majority of bike dealers are asking Cocalis when he'll make an ebike available. \"There's tremendous opportunity to get a generation of people for whom suffering isn't their thing,\" Cocalis said. \"Ebike riders get the enjoyable part of cycling without the massive suffering of climbing huge hills.\"", "49": "A growing number of U.S. bike riders are attracted to electric bikes for convenience, health benefits and their fun factor. Although ebikes first appeared in the 90s, cheaper options and longer-lasting batteries are breathing new life into the concept.\nEstablished bike companies and startups are embracing ebikes to meet demand. About 34 million ebikes were sold worldwide last year, according to data from eCycleElectric Consultants. Most were sold in Europe and China, where the bikes already have exploded in popularity. Recently, the U.S. market has grown to 263,000 bikes, a 25% gain from the prior year.\nThe industry is benefiting from improved batteries as suppliers over the years developed technology for laptops, smartphones and electric cars. In 2004, the price of batteries used on ebikes fell, spurring European sales.\nBut lower cost options are emerging, too. This month, three U.S. bikeshare companies, Motivate, LimeBike and Spin, announced electric bicycles will be added to their fleets. New York-based Jump Bikes is already operating an electric bikeshare in Washington, D.C., and is launching in San Francisco Thursday. Rides cost $2 for 30 minutes.\nThe system works like existing dockless bikeshare systems, where riders unlock bikes through a smartphone app. \"This is the beginning of a long-term shift away from regular pedal(踏板) to electric bikes,\" said Jump Bikes CEO Ryan Rzepecki. \"When people first jump on an ebike, their face lights up. It's exciting and joyful in a way that you don't get from a regular bike.\"\nTwo years ago, CEO Chris Cocalis of Pivot Cycles, which sells high-end mountain bikes, found that U.S. bike shops weren't interested in stocking ebikes. Some retailers warned Cocalis that they'd drop the brand if it came out with an electric bike.\nNow that sales are taking off, the vast majority of bike dealers are asking Cocalis when he'll make an ebike available. \"There's tremendous opportunity to get a generation of people for whom suffering isn't their thing,\" Cocalis said. \"Ebike riders get the enjoyable part of cycling without the massive suffering of climbing huge hills.\"", "50": "A growing number of U.S. bike riders are attracted to electric bikes for convenience, health benefits and their fun factor. Although ebikes first appeared in the 90s, cheaper options and longer-lasting batteries are breathing new life into the concept.\nEstablished bike companies and startups are embracing ebikes to meet demand. About 34 million ebikes were sold worldwide last year, according to data from eCycleElectric Consultants. Most were sold in Europe and China, where the bikes already have exploded in popularity. Recently, the U.S. market has grown to 263,000 bikes, a 25% gain from the prior year.\nThe industry is benefiting from improved batteries as suppliers over the years developed technology for laptops, smartphones and electric cars. In 2004, the price of batteries used on ebikes fell, spurring European sales.\nBut lower cost options are emerging, too. This month, three U.S. bikeshare companies, Motivate, LimeBike and Spin, announced electric bicycles will be added to their fleets. New York-based Jump Bikes is already operating an electric bikeshare in Washington, D.C., and is launching in San Francisco Thursday. Rides cost $2 for 30 minutes.\nThe system works like existing dockless bikeshare systems, where riders unlock bikes through a smartphone app. \"This is the beginning of a long-term shift away from regular pedal(踏板) to electric bikes,\" said Jump Bikes CEO Ryan Rzepecki. \"When people first jump on an ebike, their face lights up. It's exciting and joyful in a way that you don't get from a regular bike.\"\nTwo years ago, CEO Chris Cocalis of Pivot Cycles, which sells high-end mountain bikes, found that U.S. bike shops weren't interested in stocking ebikes. Some retailers warned Cocalis that they'd drop the brand if it came out with an electric bike.\nNow that sales are taking off, the vast majority of bike dealers are asking Cocalis when he'll make an ebike available. \"There's tremendous opportunity to get a generation of people for whom suffering isn't their thing,\" Cocalis said. \"Ebike riders get the enjoyable part of cycling without the massive suffering of climbing huge hills.\"", "51": "The terms \"global warming\" and \"climate change\" are used by many, seemingly interchangeably. But do they really mean the same thing?\nScientists shaped the history of the terms while attempting to accurately describe how humans continue to alter the planet. Later, political strategists adopted the terms to influence public opinion.\nIn 1975, geochemist Wallace Broecker introduced the term \"climate change\" in an article published by Science. In 1979, a National Academy of Sciences report used the term \"global warming\" to define increases in the Earth's average surface temperature, while \"climate change\" more broadly referred to the numerous effects of this increase, such as sea-level rise and ocean acidification(酸化).\nDuring the following decades, some industrialists and politicians launched a campaign to sow doubt in the minds of the American public about the ability of fossil-fuel use, deforestation and other human activities to influence the planet's climate.\nWord use played a critical role in developing that doubt. For example, the language and polls expert Frank Luntz wrote a memo encouraging the use of \"climate change\" because the phrase sounded less scary than \"global warming,\" reported the Guardian.\nHowever, Luntz's recommendation wasn't necessary. A Google Ngram Viewer chart shows that by 1993 climate change was already more commonly used in books than global warming. By the end of the next decade both words were used more frequently, and climate change was used nearly twice as often as global warming.\nNASA used the term \"climate change\" because it more accurately reflects the wide range of changes to the planet caused by increasing amounts of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.\nThe debate isn't new. A century ago, chemist Svante Arrhenius started one of the first debates over the potential for humans to influence the planet's climate. Arrhenius calculated the capability of carbon dioxide to trap heat in the Earth's atmosphere, but other chemists disagreed. Some argued that humans weren't producing enough greenhouse gases, while others claimed the effects would be tiny. Now, of course, we know that whatever you call it, human behavior is warming the planet, with grave consequences ahead.", "52": "The terms \"global warming\" and \"climate change\" are used by many, seemingly interchangeably. But do they really mean the same thing?\nScientists shaped the history of the terms while attempting to accurately describe how humans continue to alter the planet. Later, political strategists adopted the terms to influence public opinion.\nIn 1975, geochemist Wallace Broecker introduced the term \"climate change\" in an article published by Science. In 1979, a National Academy of Sciences report used the term \"global warming\" to define increases in the Earth's average surface temperature, while \"climate change\" more broadly referred to the numerous effects of this increase, such as sea-level rise and ocean acidification(酸化).\nDuring the following decades, some industrialists and politicians launched a campaign to sow doubt in the minds of the American public about the ability of fossil-fuel use, deforestation and other human activities to influence the planet's climate.\nWord use played a critical role in developing that doubt. For example, the language and polls expert Frank Luntz wrote a memo encouraging the use of \"climate change\" because the phrase sounded less scary than \"global warming,\" reported the Guardian.\nHowever, Luntz's recommendation wasn't necessary. A Google Ngram Viewer chart shows that by 1993 climate change was already more commonly used in books than global warming. By the end of the next decade both words were used more frequently, and climate change was used nearly twice as often as global warming.\nNASA used the term \"climate change\" because it more accurately reflects the wide range of changes to the planet caused by increasing amounts of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.\nThe debate isn't new. A century ago, chemist Svante Arrhenius started one of the first debates over the potential for humans to influence the planet's climate. Arrhenius calculated the capability of carbon dioxide to trap heat in the Earth's atmosphere, but other chemists disagreed. Some argued that humans weren't producing enough greenhouse gases, while others claimed the effects would be tiny. Now, of course, we know that whatever you call it, human behavior is warming the planet, with grave consequences ahead.", "53": "The terms \"global warming\" and \"climate change\" are used by many, seemingly interchangeably. But do they really mean the same thing?\nScientists shaped the history of the terms while attempting to accurately describe how humans continue to alter the planet. Later, political strategists adopted the terms to influence public opinion.\nIn 1975, geochemist Wallace Broecker introduced the term \"climate change\" in an article published by Science. In 1979, a National Academy of Sciences report used the term \"global warming\" to define increases in the Earth's average surface temperature, while \"climate change\" more broadly referred to the numerous effects of this increase, such as sea-level rise and ocean acidification(酸化).\nDuring the following decades, some industrialists and politicians launched a campaign to sow doubt in the minds of the American public about the ability of fossil-fuel use, deforestation and other human activities to influence the planet's climate.\nWord use played a critical role in developing that doubt. For example, the language and polls expert Frank Luntz wrote a memo encouraging the use of \"climate change\" because the phrase sounded less scary than \"global warming,\" reported the Guardian.\nHowever, Luntz's recommendation wasn't necessary. A Google Ngram Viewer chart shows that by 1993 climate change was already more commonly used in books than global warming. By the end of the next decade both words were used more frequently, and climate change was used nearly twice as often as global warming.\nNASA used the term \"climate change\" because it more accurately reflects the wide range of changes to the planet caused by increasing amounts of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.\nThe debate isn't new. A century ago, chemist Svante Arrhenius started one of the first debates over the potential for humans to influence the planet's climate. Arrhenius calculated the capability of carbon dioxide to trap heat in the Earth's atmosphere, but other chemists disagreed. Some argued that humans weren't producing enough greenhouse gases, while others claimed the effects would be tiny. Now, of course, we know that whatever you call it, human behavior is warming the planet, with grave consequences ahead.", "54": "The terms \"global warming\" and \"climate change\" are used by many, seemingly interchangeably. But do they really mean the same thing?\nScientists shaped the history of the terms while attempting to accurately describe how humans continue to alter the planet. Later, political strategists adopted the terms to influence public opinion.\nIn 1975, geochemist Wallace Broecker introduced the term \"climate change\" in an article published by Science. In 1979, a National Academy of Sciences report used the term \"global warming\" to define increases in the Earth's average surface temperature, while \"climate change\" more broadly referred to the numerous effects of this increase, such as sea-level rise and ocean acidification(酸化).\nDuring the following decades, some industrialists and politicians launched a campaign to sow doubt in the minds of the American public about the ability of fossil-fuel use, deforestation and other human activities to influence the planet's climate.\nWord use played a critical role in developing that doubt. For example, the language and polls expert Frank Luntz wrote a memo encouraging the use of \"climate change\" because the phrase sounded less scary than \"global warming,\" reported the Guardian.\nHowever, Luntz's recommendation wasn't necessary. A Google Ngram Viewer chart shows that by 1993 climate change was already more commonly used in books than global warming. By the end of the next decade both words were used more frequently, and climate change was used nearly twice as often as global warming.\nNASA used the term \"climate change\" because it more accurately reflects the wide range of changes to the planet caused by increasing amounts of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.\nThe debate isn't new. A century ago, chemist Svante Arrhenius started one of the first debates over the potential for humans to influence the planet's climate. Arrhenius calculated the capability of carbon dioxide to trap heat in the Earth's atmosphere, but other chemists disagreed. Some argued that humans weren't producing enough greenhouse gases, while others claimed the effects would be tiny. Now, of course, we know that whatever you call it, human behavior is warming the planet, with grave consequences ahead.", "55": "The terms \"global warming\" and \"climate change\" are used by many, seemingly interchangeably. But do they really mean the same thing?\nScientists shaped the history of the terms while attempting to accurately describe how humans continue to alter the planet. Later, political strategists adopted the terms to influence public opinion.\nIn 1975, geochemist Wallace Broecker introduced the term \"climate change\" in an article published by Science. In 1979, a National Academy of Sciences report used the term \"global warming\" to define increases in the Earth's average surface temperature, while \"climate change\" more broadly referred to the numerous effects of this increase, such as sea-level rise and ocean acidification(酸化).\nDuring the following decades, some industrialists and politicians launched a campaign to sow doubt in the minds of the American public about the ability of fossil-fuel use, deforestation and other human activities to influence the planet's climate.\nWord use played a critical role in developing that doubt. For example, the language and polls expert Frank Luntz wrote a memo encouraging the use of \"climate change\" because the phrase sounded less scary than \"global warming,\" reported the Guardian.\nHowever, Luntz's recommendation wasn't necessary. A Google Ngram Viewer chart shows that by 1993 climate change was already more commonly used in books than global warming. By the end of the next decade both words were used more frequently, and climate change was used nearly twice as often as global warming.\nNASA used the term \"climate change\" because it more accurately reflects the wide range of changes to the planet caused by increasing amounts of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.\nThe debate isn't new. A century ago, chemist Svante Arrhenius started one of the first debates over the potential for humans to influence the planet's climate. Arrhenius calculated the capability of carbon dioxide to trap heat in the Earth's atmosphere, but other chemists disagreed. Some argued that humans weren't producing enough greenhouse gases, while others claimed the effects would be tiny. Now, of course, we know that whatever you call it, human behavior is warming the planet, with grave consequences ahead."}, "questions": [{"id": "2020-12-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automation", "B) concerns", "C) enormously", "D) fantastic", "E) fascinated", "F) feature", "G) flexible", "H) inevitably", "I) interaction", "J) leaning", "K) matters", "L) moving", "M) promised", "N) shape", "O) spared"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) matters\n动词\n【词性槽】any disruption + ___ 缺谓语动词\n【依据】matter 作不及物动词 = 「至关重要」\n承接前句 The things people make, and the way they make them, determine how\ncities grow and decline, and influence how empires rise and fall.——既然制造决定城\n市与帝国兴衰，那么对世界工厂的任何破坏「就是大事/至关重要」，matters 作不及物动\n词正是这个意思。\n【竞争词】B) concerns concerns 作动词通常需要宾语\nconcerns 表「涉及/使担心」时一般是及物动词（concerns sth./sb.），独立作谓语收尾不自\n然；而 matter 是典型的不及物动词，可直接以句号结尾。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automation", "B) concerns", "C) enormously", "D) fantastic", "E) fascinated", "F) feature", "G) flexible", "H) inevitably", "I) interaction", "J) leaning", "K) matters", "L) moving", "M) promised", "N) shape", "O) spared"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) flexible\n形容词\n【词性槽】并列三个 more+形容词，唯一槽位要形容词\n【依据】数字化工厂的核心卖点是「更灵活」\nFactories are being digitised, filled with new sensors and new computers to make\nthem quicker, more __(27)__, and more efficient. 三个并列形容词在描述「数字化升级后\n工厂的优点」，「更快、更灵活、更高效」是制造业话题里最常见的三件套。\n【竞争词】D) fantastic fantastic 是评价性形容词，不与 more 自然搭配\nfantastic 本身就含有「极好的」最高级语义，英文里几乎不说 more fantastic；而且这里要的\n是可度量的属性（快/灵活/高效），不是主观评价。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automation", "B) concerns", "C) enormously", "D) fantastic", "E) fascinated", "F) feature", "G) flexible", "H) inevitably", "I) interaction", "J) leaning", "K) matters", "L) moving", "M) promised", "N) shape", "O) spared"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) promised\n动词\n【词性槽】have long ___ 完成时缺过去分词\n【依据】promise a world 表「许诺/预示一个世界」\n3D 打印机长期以来一直「许诺/预示」一个「任何东西都能在任何地方制造出来」的世界\n——promise 在英文里有「让人期待、预示前景」的引申义，与紧随其后的 That vision is\n__(29)__ closer to reality.「那个愿景正在更接近现实」完美呼应：先 promise（许诺愿\n景）→ 后 vision（愿景）。\n【竞争词】E) fascinated fascinate 的宾语应是「人」而非「世界」\nfascinate 表「使……着迷」，宾语通常是人（fascinated us）；3D printers have long\nfascinated a world 把世界当宾语既不自然，也无法引出后句「那个 vision 接近现实」的呼应。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— fascinated 形态对、搭配错。fascinated 是 -ed 过去分\n词，能放进 have long ___ 的完成时框架，主体考生若只看形态极易选错；防坑点是看后面\n宾语 a world——fascinate 的宾语应是人，而 promise 才能带 a world 这种「愿景」类宾\n语，并与下句 vision 形成呼应链。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automation", "B) concerns", "C) enormously", "D) fantastic", "E) fascinated", "F) feature", "G) flexible", "H) inevitably", "I) interaction", "J) leaning", "K) matters", "L) moving", "M) promised", "N) shape", "O) spared"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) moving\n动词\n【词性槽】is + ___ closer，be + V-ing 进行时\n【依据】moving closer to 是「越来越接近」的固定表达\n「is moving closer to reality」是英文里描述「愿景/目标正在越来越接近现实」的固定说\n法，承接 28 题的 promised a world：先许诺一个愿景，如今这个愿景 is moving closer\nto reality.。\n【竞争词】J) leaning lean 表「倾斜/倾向」，方向感不对\nlean closer 字面是「向前倾得更近」，多用于身体动作；表达抽象的「愿景接近现实」用 move\ncloser to 才地道，leaning closer to reality 不是英文里的常见搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automation", "B) concerns", "C) enormously", "D) fantastic", "E) fascinated", "F) feature", "G) flexible", "H) inevitably", "I) interaction", "J) leaning", "K) matters", "L) moving", "M) promised", "N) shape", "O) spared"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) spared\n动词\n【词性槽】Humans will be ___ + 名词，被动语态\n【依据】be spared sth. = 被免去某事\nspare sb. sth. 表「使某人免遭某事」，被动 be spared sth. 表「被免去某事」；这里说人\n类将被免去那些「脏、重复、危险的工作」——正好对应第二段开头机器人接管这些活的\n语境。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automation", "B) concerns", "C) enormously", "D) fantastic", "E) fascinated", "F) feature", "G) flexible", "H) inevitably", "I) interaction", "J) leaning", "K) matters", "L) moving", "M) promised", "N) shape", "O) spared"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) feature\n名词\n【词性槽】a + ___ of factory life，冠词后必为名词\n【依据】a feature of … 是固定搭配，表「…的一大特征」\n句意是「肮脏、重复、危险的工作长期以来是工厂生活的一大__」，a feature of factory\nlife 正好等于「工厂生活的一个特征」，与 have long been（长期以来一直是）的时间感\n完美贴合。\n【竞争词】I) interaction interaction 指「互动」，与「脏活累活」语义不搭\ninteraction 强调双方相互作用，把「dirty, repetitive, and dangerous jobs」称为 a\ninteraction of factory life 在语义上讲不通，工作本身不是「互动」。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automation", "B) concerns", "C) enormously", "D) fantastic", "E) fascinated", "F) feature", "G) flexible", "H) inevitably", "I) interaction", "J) leaning", "K) matters", "L) moving", "M) promised", "N) shape", "O) spared"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) inevitably\n副词\n【词性槽】副词修饰整句/动词 means\n【依据】「效率提高」与「人变少」是必然因果\n句意是「效率提高 __ 意味着同样的工作用更少的人就能完成」，逻辑上是一个必然推论，\n而非强调程度。Greater efficiency __(32)__ means fewer people can do the same\nwork. 用 inevitably（必然地、不可避免地）来标记这种因果必然性最贴。\n【竞争词】C) enormously enormously 强调「程度大」而非「必然性」\nenormously means 在英文里几乎不成立——它通常修饰量/程度（如 enormously\nexpensive），与 mean 这种逻辑判断动词搭配不自然；这里也不是讨论「效率多么巨大地意味\n着」，而是「必然意味着」。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 C) —— enormously 词性对、逻辑错。enormously 和 inevitably\n都是副词，都能塞进 Greater efficiency __(32)__ means；防坑点是判断这是「程度」还是\n「必然性」——mean 这种逻辑判断动词要的是表必然性的副词（inevitably），而非表程\n度的（enormously）。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automation", "B) concerns", "C) enormously", "D) fantastic", "E) fascinated", "F) feature", "G) flexible", "H) inevitably", "I) interaction", "J) leaning", "K) matters", "L) moving", "M) promised", "N) shape", "O) spared"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) automation\n名词\n【词性槽】and 连接的并列名词，与 robots 同列\n【依据】automation and robots 是制造业话题黄金搭配\n工厂主担心缺技术工人，把「自动化和机器人」视作 a solution——automation 与\nrobots 同属「替代人工的技术」范畴，是四级与雅思阅读里描述未来工厂的高频搭档。\n【竞争词】I) interaction interaction 不是替代人工的技术手段\ninteraction 指「互动」，并不构成 a solution（解决缺工的方案）；把 interaction and\nrobots 并列既不合搭配，也违背「缺人 → 用技术替人」的因果。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automation", "B) concerns", "C) enormously", "D) fantastic", "E) fascinated", "F) feature", "G) flexible", "H) inevitably", "I) interaction", "J) leaning", "K) matters", "L) moving", "M) promised", "N) shape", "O) spared"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) fantastic\n形容词\n【词性槽】a + ___ + opportunity，冠词后名词前作定语\n【依据】评价 opportunity 用褒义强词「绝佳的」\n经济学家说「这是一个 __ 的机会去让世界更好」，结合后文 if we can, it will have\ntremendous significance. 的强烈褒义，用 fantastic（绝佳的）来评价 opportunity 在语\n义强度与感情色彩上都贴合。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) automation", "B) concerns", "C) enormously", "D) fantastic", "E) fascinated", "F) feature", "G) flexible", "H) inevitably", "I) interaction", "J) leaning", "K) matters", "L) moving", "M) promised", "N) shape", "O) spared"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) shape\n动词\n【词性槽】to ___ that system，不定式 to 后接动词原形\n【依据】shape the system = 塑造这个体系\nshape 作动词表「塑造、使成形」，shape the system differently 即「以不同的方式塑造\n这个体系」，正好承接 That's an opportunity to __(35)__ that system differently, and if\nwe can, it will have tremendous significance. 的「重大意义」——主动重塑制造业是经\n济学家眼里的重大机会。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Lunch containers were not necessary for school kids in cities.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】城市学生 + 不需要午餐容器\n【定位】City kids, on the other hand, went home for lunch and came back. Since they rarely\ncarried a meal, the few metal lunch buckets on the market were mainly for tradesmen and\nfactory workers.\n另一方面，城里的孩子中午回家吃饭再返校。由于他们很少带饭，市面上仅有的几款金属午\n餐桶主要面向手工业者和工厂工人。\n【改写】Lunch containers were not necessary ↔ they rarely carried a meal\n频率极低 = 不必要\nschool kids in cities ↔ City kids\n群体词原样复现\n【辨邻】 E 本段主语锁定在 You had kids in rural areas——讨论的是乡村孩子带饭的传统，而题\n干 school kids in cities 限定群体是城市，群体相反，不能匹配。\nYou had kids in rural areas who couldn't go home from school for lunch, so bringing your lunch\nwrapped in a cloth, in oiled paper, in a little wooden box or something like that was a very long-\nstanding rural tradition", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Putting TV characters on lunch boxes proved an effective marketing strategy.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】电视角色印盒 + 营销奏效\n【定位】It was a major \"Ah-ha!\" moment, and a wave of other manufacturers jumped on board to\ncapitalize on new TV shows and movies. \"It was a great marketing tool because kids were\ntaking that TV show to school with them, and then when they got home they had them\ncaptured back on TV,\" he says.\n这是一个重大的「恍然大悟」时刻，一大波厂商纷纷跟上，借新电视节目和电影大做文章。\n「这是一个绝佳的营销工具，因为孩子们把那部电视节目带进了学校，回家后又能在电视上\n重新看见它，」他说。\n【改写】Putting TV characters on lunch boxes ↔\neverything that was on television ended up on a lunch box\n动作改写：印 = 落在盒子上\neffective marketing strategy ↔ great marketing tool\n近义形容词 + 同根名词\nproved an effective marketing strategy ↔ It was a major \"Ah-ha!\" moment\n结果性评价 ↔ 顿悟式肯定\n【辨邻】 I I 段讲的是 Aladdin 因为盒子太耐用、销售吃亏，所以才想到「借电视红人印盒」这一\n点子——内容停留在动机和点子本身，没出现销量爆发、同行跟随这类「结果性」信\n息，不能承担 proved an effective marketing strategy 这一对「结果」的判断。\nexecutives at Aladdin hit on an idea that would harness the newfound popularity of television. They\ncovered lunch boxes with striking red paint and added a picture of TV and radio cowboy Hopalong\nCassidy on the front", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Smaller lunch boxes are preferred because they fit easily into backpacks.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】更小盒 + 塞进书包\n【定位】That is, in part, why smaller and softer containers have taken off—they fit into backpacks.\n这在一定程度上解释了为什么更小、更软的容器开始流行——它们能塞进书包。\n【改写】Smaller lunch boxes are preferred ↔ smaller and softer containers have taken off\n被动偏好 ↔ 主动走红\nfit easily into backpacks ↔ they fit into backpacks\n几乎原样复现\n【辨邻】 N N 段讲塑料盒过渡到布袋、tiffin、bento 的整体演变，并引用 I don't think the\nheyday 的判断，但并未出现「书包」这一原因抓手，无法承担 fit easily into\nbackpacks 这一具体因果。\nPlastic boxes changed to lined cloth sacks, and eventually, globalism brought tiffin containers from\nIndia and bento boxes from Japan", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Lunch boxes have evolved along with the transformation of schools.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】随学校转型而演变\n【定位】As schools have changed in the past century, the midday meal container has evolved right\nalong with them.\n随着过去一个世纪里学校发生变化，午间饭盒也随之一同演变。\n【改写】Lunch boxes have evolved along with the transformation of schools ↔\nAs schools have changed in the past century, the midday meal container has evolved right\nalong with them\n句式重构 + 词性变换\n【辨邻】 G G 段列举二战后学校的几项具体变化 More women joined the workforce、Small\nschools consolidated into larger ones、the National School Lunch Act in\n1946，这些都是「学校变了」的事实例证，未给出「午餐盒与学校共同演变」这一全\n景式总论，承载不了 Q39 的判断。\nAfter World War II, a bunch of changes reshaped schools—and lunches\nN N 段虽然有 I think it has evolved 这种「演变」措辞，但开头限定在 The last few\ndecades have brought a new lunch box revolution——只是近几十年的局部演\n变，且并未把「学校转型」作为驱动因素，无法承担 transformation of schools 这一\n总论。\nThe last few decades have brought a new lunch box revolution, of sorts", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Around the beginning of the nineteen fifties, some school kids started to use metal meal containers.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】1950 年前后 + 学生用金属饭盒\n【定位】And then everything changed in the year of 1950. You might as well call it the Year of the\nLunch Box, thanks in large part to a genius move by a Nashville-based manufacturer, Aladdin\nIndustries. The company already made square metal meal containers, the kind workers\ncarried, and some had started to show up in the hands of school kids.\n1950 年一切都变了。你不妨把那一年称为「午餐盒元年」，这在很大程度上要归功于位于\n纳什维尔的制造商 Aladdin Industries 的一次妙招。这家公司当时已经生产方形金属饭盒\n——就是工人们用的那种——其中一些已开始出现在学生手里。\n【改写】Around the beginning of the nineteen fifties ↔ in the year of 1950\n年份精确对应\nsome school kids started to use metal meal containers ↔\nsome had started to show up in the hands of school kids\n学生使用 ↔ 出现在学生手中\n【辨邻】 I I 段讲的是金属盒因为太耐用，于是被 covered with striking red paint 并加上\nHopalong Cassidy 头像——这是金属盒进入学生手之后的下一步动作，承载不了\nsome school kids started to use 这一「刚开始用」的语义。\nThey covered lunch boxes with striking red paint and added a picture of TV and radio cowboy\nHopalong Cassidy on the front", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "School kids are eager to get a new lunch box every year to stay in fashion.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】每年换新盒 + 追潮流\n【定位】Kids would beg for a new lunch box every year to keep up with the newest characters, even if\ntheir old lunchbox was perfectly usable.\n孩子们会每年央求换一只新午餐盒，好跟上最新角色，哪怕旧盒还完全能用。\n【改写】eager to get a new lunch box every year ↔ Kids would beg for a new lunch box every year\n渴望 ↔ 央求\nstay in fashion ↔ keep up with the newest characters\n上位概念 ↔ 下位具体\n【辨邻】 J J 段主体是厂商和博物馆创办人的话 everything that was on television ended up\non a lunch box，讲的是厂商如何利用 TV 角色，没有给出「孩子每年换新盒以追潮\n流」这一行为描述，承载不了 eager to get a new lunch box every year。\na wave of other manufacturers jumped on board to capitalize on new TV shows and movies", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Rural kids used to walk a long way to school in the old days.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】乡村孩子 + 步行很远去学校 + 旧时代\n【定位】As grandparents have been saying for generations, kids would travel miles to school in the\ncountryside (often on foot).\n正如祖辈们世代以来反复念叨的：乡下孩子上学常常要走好几英里路（往往是步行）。\n【改写】Rural kids ↔ kids would travel miles to school in the countryside\n形容词 ↔ 地点状语\nwalk a long way to school ↔\nkids would travel miles to school in the countryside (often on foot)\n动作 = 距离 + 方式 组合\nin the old days ↔ Let's start back at the beginning of the 20th century\n宽泛时代词 ↔ 具体世纪\n【辨邻】 E E 段也涉及 rural kids，但内容是 bringing your lunch wrapped in a cloth, in oiled\npaper, in a little wooden box 这一中午带饭传统，没有触及「步行长距离上学」这一\n具体动作，无法承担 walk a long way to school。\nYou had kids in rural areas who couldn't go home from school for lunch", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The author was proud of using a lunch box in her childhood.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】作者本人 + 童年用盒很自豪\n【定位】My lunch box was one of my first prized possessions, a proud statement to everyone in my\nkindergarten: \"I love Mermaid-Ariel on my lunch box.\"\n我的午餐盒是我最早的珍宝之一，是我向幼儿园里每个人发出的骄傲宣言：「我爱我盒子上\n的美人鱼艾莉儿。」\n【改写】The author was proud of using a lunch box ↔\nMy lunch box was one of my first prized possessions, a proud statement to everyone in my\nkindergarten\n同根形容词 + 自豪情绪的直接吐露\nin her childhood ↔ in my kindergarten\n上位时代词 ↔ 下位时段词\n【辨邻】 B B 段虽然也是作者本人回忆，但讲的是 I needed the newest red plastic box 这一升\n级动作和 I bet you loved your first lunchbox, too 这一对读者的猜测，没有出现\nproud / pride 这种自豪情绪，无法承担 was proud 这一情感判断。\nThat bulky container served me well through my first and second grades, until the live-action\nversion of 101 Dalmatians hit theaters, and I needed the newest red plastic box", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "The most probable reason for the popularity of plastic lunch boxes is that they are less expensive.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】塑料盒流行 + 最可能原因是便宜\n【定位】The first—and most likely—is that plastic had simply become cheaper.\n第一种——也是可能性最大的——解释是：塑料只是变得更便宜了。\n【改写】popularity of plastic lunch boxes ↔ plastic took over\n走红 ↔ 取代\nThe most probable reason ↔ The first—and most likely\n最高级近义\nless expensive ↔ plastic had simply become cheaper\n比较级近义\n【辨邻】 N N 段虽然提到 Plastic boxes changed to lined cloth sacks 这一塑料盒的后续演\n变，但全段未给出「塑料盒为什么流行 / 取代金属」的原因，无法承担 The most\nprobable reason for the popularity of plastic lunch boxes 这一对「原因」的\n判断。\nPlastic boxes changed to lined cloth sacks, and eventually, globalism brought tiffin containers from\nIndia and bento boxes from Japan", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "The durability of metal meal containers benefited consumers.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】金属饭盒耐用 + 利于消费者\n【定位】But these containers were really durable, lasting years on end. That was great for the\nconsumer, not so much for the manufacturer.\n但这些饭盒非常耐用，能用上好多年。对消费者来说这是好事，对厂商就不那么好了。\n【改写】The durability of metal meal containers ↔\nthese containers were really durable, lasting years on end\n名词化 ↔ 形容词谓语\nbenefited consumers ↔ That was great for the consumer\n动词 ↔ 形容词短语\n【辨邻】 H H 段虽然提到 square metal meal containers，但全段聚焦「1950 年金属盒首次进\n入学生手」这一节点，未涉及 durable / great for the consumer 这一属性 + 评价判\n断，承担不了 Q45 的「耐用利消费者」语义。\nThe company already made square metal meal containers, the kind workers carried, and some had\nstarted to show up in the hands of school kids", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about ebikes?", "options": ["A) Their health benefits and fun values outweigh their cost.", "B) They did not catch public attention in the United States until the 1990s.", "C) They did not become popular until the emergence of improved batteries.", "D) Their widespread use is attributable to people's environmental awareness."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about ebikes? 没有 infer/imply/mainly about，\n问的是文中陈述的客观信息，四个选项各对应一处事实，属于综合性细节判断。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the passage about ebikes? 是常规疑问句：主干是 What do we\nlearn，about ebikes 是介词短语限定话题，from the passage 提示答题范围在通篇，无\n陷阱结构。\n【定位】Although ebikes first appeared in the 90s, cheaper options and longer-lasting batteries are\nbreathing new life into the concept.\n尽管电动自行车早在 90 年代就出现了，但更便宜的款式和更耐用的电池正在为这个概念注\n入新的生命力。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文只说 convenience, health benefits and their fun factor 是吸引人骑电动车的\n几个并列原因，并没有把「健康与乐趣」与「成本」放在一起比，更没说「前者超过\n后者」。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 ebikes first appeared in the 90s 是说「90 年代才首次出现」，没有「在那之前\n美国人不知道、之后才关注」的意思；而 They did not catch public attention in the\nUnited States until the 1990s. 把「出现」偷换成了「引起公众关注」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nAlthough ebikes first appeared in the 90s, cheaper options and longer-lasting batteries are\nbreathing new life into the concept.\n↔ They did not become popular until the emergence of improved batteries.\n预判的「电池变好才流行」与 They did not become popular until the emergence of\nimproved batteries. 的「直到电池改良才流行」语义完全一致；首段又用 The\nindustry is benefiting from improved batteries 强化「电池→产业受益」的因果，可\n二次确认。\nD) 无中生有\n全文只字未提 environmental awareness（环保意识），原文给出的流行原因是\nconvenience, health benefits and their fun factor 与电池改进，没有任何句子把「环\n保」列为推动因素。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What brought about the boost in ebike sales in Europe at the beginning of the century?", "options": ["A) Updated technology of bike manufacture.", "B) The falling prices of ebike batteries.", "C) Changed fashion in short-distance travel.", "D) The rising costs for making electric cars."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What brought about the boost in ebike sales in Europe at the beginning of the\ncentury? 是 What brought about 句式，问引起某事件的具体原因，是一道指向明确的因\n果细节题。\n【拆句】What brought about the boost in ebike sales in Europe at the beginning of the\ncentury? 主干是 What brought about the boost，in ebike sales 修饰 boost，in\nEurope 与 at the beginning of the century 是两个并列状语，分别给出地点与时间，结构\n清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】In 2004, the price of batteries used on ebikes fell, spurring European sales.\n2004 年，用于电动自行车的电池价格下降，促使欧洲销量大增。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文谈的是 The industry is benefiting from improved batteries as suppliers over\nthe years developed technology for laptops, smartphones and electric cars.——\n技术进步发生在电池领域（且来自笔记本、手机、电动汽车），不是 bike manufacture\n（自行车制造）。\nB) ✓ 正确\nIn 2004, the price of batteries used on ebikes fell, spurring European sales. ↔\nThe falling prices of ebike batteries.\n预判「电池价格下降」与 The falling prices of ebike batteries. 完全一致；spurring\n这个分词正好把「价格下降」与「欧洲销量」用因果绑在一起，无需推理。\nC) 无中生有\n原文从未提及 short-distance travel（短途出行）这个概念，更未把它和欧洲销量增长\n挂钩；考生若凭「电动车=城市短途」的常识硬选，就掉进无中生有陷阱。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文只说电池技术是从 laptops, smartphones and electric cars 这些领域延伸过来\n的，并没说「电动汽车制造成本上升」导致电动自行车在欧洲热销；选项把不相关的两\n件事强行拼接。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is the prospect of the bike industry according to Ryan Rzepecki?", "options": ["A) More will be invested in bike battery research.", "B) The sales of ebikes will increase.", "C) It will profit from ebike sharing.", "D) It will make a difference in people's daily lives."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is the prospect of the bike industry according to Ryan Rzepecki? 用 according\nto + 人名，问的是他对前景的具体看法，答案应在他的原话里直接拿到，是定向细节题。\n【拆句】What is the prospect of the bike industry according to Ryan Rzepecki? 是简单疑问\n句：主干 What is the prospect，of the bike industry 修饰 prospect，according to\nRyan Rzepecki 限定信息源，无陷阱。\n【定位】This is the beginning of a long-term shift away from regular pedal(踏板) to electric bikes.\n这是从普通脚踏自行车向电动自行车长期转变的开端。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nCEO 原话谈的是「骑行体验」和「长期转向」，与「投资电池研究」无关；原文第 3 段\n提到电池技术从其他行业受益，但从未说会加大对电池研究的投入。\nB) ✓ 正确\nThis is the beginning of a long-term shift away from regular pedal(踏板) to electric bikes. ↔\nThe sales of ebikes will increase.\n预判的「销量会持续增长」与 The sales of ebikes will increase. 在语义上一致；且第 2\n段已铺垫 the U.S. market has grown to 263,000 bikes, a 25% gain from the prior\nyear，这种「长期转向」在 CEO 嘴里说出，结合销量背景，必然指向「销量上升」。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文确实在 P4 P5 介绍了 Jump Bikes 等公司的共享业务，但 CEO 的原话谈的是「骑\n行体验与长期转向」，没有说整个产业会「靠共享盈利」。\nD) 过度推断\nCEO 原话只说「是一次长期转向的开端」，并未上升到「改变人们日常生活」的层级；\n考生若把「长期转向」凭印象拔高，就会过度推断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What prevented Chris Cocalis from developing ebikes sooner?", "options": ["A) Retailers' refusal to deal in ebikes.", "B) High profits from conventional bikes.", "C) Users' concern about risks of ebike riding.", "D) His focus on selling costly mountain bikes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What prevented Chris Cocalis from developing ebikes sooner? 问的是阻碍发生在过去\n的具体事实，需要在原文找到对应的客观陈述，属典型细节题。\n【拆句】What prevented Chris Cocalis from developing ebikes sooner? 主干是 What\nprevented Chris Cocalis，from developing ebikes sooner 是 prevent sb from doing\nsth 的固定搭配，意为「阻止他更早开发电动车」；考生若没掌握这个搭配，容易把\nprevent ... from 理解反，需特别留意。\n【定位】Two years ago, CEO Chris Cocalis of Pivot Cycles, which sells high-end mountain bikes,\nfound that U.S. bike shops weren't interested in stocking ebikes. Some retailers warned\nCocalis that they'd drop the brand if it came out with an electric bike.\n两年前，主营高端山地车的 Pivot Cycles 公司 CEO Chris Cocalis 发现，美国的自行车商\n店并没有兴趣进电动自行车。一些零售商警告 Cocalis，如果他出电动车，他们就停售他的\n品牌。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nSome retailers warned Cocalis that they'd drop the brand if it came out with an electric bike. ↔\nRetailers' refusal to deal in ebikes.\n预判的「零售商不愿进货+威胁停售」与 Retailers' refusal to deal in ebikes. 完全等\n值；refusal to deal in（拒绝经营）是 weren't interested in stocking + warned ...\nthey'd drop the brand 的概括。\nB) 无中生有\n原文只说 Pivot Cycles, which sells high-end mountain bikes，介绍公司业务，未提\n及「传统车带来高利润」或他舍不得放弃高利润；考生凭「高端=高利润」的常识硬补，\n是无中生有。\nC) 无中生有\n全文从未提到 risk of ebike riding（电动车骑行风险）或 users' concern（用户担\n忧）；阻碍来自零售商，而不是用户的安全顾虑。\nD) 偷换概念\nPivot Cycles, which sells high-end mountain bikes 只是介绍公司业务的非限制性定\n语从句，是背景；并不是「他专注卖高端山地车」导致他没做电动车——阻碍来自零售\n商，不来自他自己。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What makes Chris Cocalis believe there is a greater opportunity for ebike sales?", "options": ["A) The further lowering of ebike prices.", "B) The public's concern for their health.", "C) The increasing interest in mountain climbing.", "D) The younger generation's pursuit of comfortable riding."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What makes Chris Cocalis believe there is a greater opportunity for ebike sales? 问的\n是导致他形成某观点的具体理由，答案应在他原话里给出，属于定向细节题。\n【拆句】What makes Chris Cocalis believe there is a greater opportunity for ebike sales? 主干\nWhat makes Chris Cocalis believe ...，make sb do sth 表「使某人……」，believe 后接\nthat 从句（that 省略），从句主干是 there is a greater opportunity，for ebike sales 修\n饰 opportunity；结构层级清晰，但 make sb believe 后接长从句，基础薄弱考生可能把\nbelieve 误当成名词，需提醒。\n【定位】There's tremendous opportunity to get a generation of people for whom suffering isn't their\nthing. Ebike riders get the enjoyable part of cycling without the massive suffering of climbing\nhuge hills.\n存在一个巨大的机会，能争取到一整代不愿吃苦的人。骑电动车的人可以享受骑行的乐趣，\n而不必承受爬大坡那种痛苦折磨。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n电动车价格下降在 cheaper options 与 the price of batteries used on ebikes fell 中\n出现过，但 Cocalis 在 P7 谈到机会时，理由是「一代人不愿吃苦」，与价格无关。\nB) 答非所问\nhealth benefits 确实是电动车整体吸引力之一，但 Cocalis 在 P7 谈机会时，给出的理\n由是「不愿吃苦」，与公众健康关注无关。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 without the massive suffering of climbing huge hills 说的是骑电动车可以免去\n爬坡的折磨——也就是说人们想避开爬坡，而不是对爬山兴趣上升。\nD) ✓ 正确\nThere's tremendous opportunity to get a generation of people for whom suffering isn't their\nthing.\n↔ The younger generation's pursuit of comfortable riding.\n预判的「新一代不愿吃苦、追求舒适」与 The younger generation's pursuit of\ncomfortable riding. 完全对应；原话两句一起构成完整因果：「有一代人不愿吃苦」+\n「电动车让骑行享受而不痛苦」——这正是他看到机会的依据。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "Why did politicians use the two terms \"global warming\" and \"climate change\"?", "options": ["A) To sway public opinion of the impact of human activities on Earth.", "B) To more accurately describe the consequences of human activities.", "C) To win more popular votes in their campaign activities.", "D) To assure the public of the safety of existing industries."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why did politicians use the two terms 问的是政客使用这两个词的具体原因，属对文中明\n示原因的事实性提问。\n【拆句】题干 Why did politicians use the two terms \"global warming\" and \"climate change\"?\n主干是 \"Why did politicians use the two terms\"，引号里的两个术语只是 the two terms\n的同位补充，句式平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Later, political strategists adopted the terms to influence public opinion.\n后来，政治策略家们采用这些术语，是为了影响公众舆论。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nto influence public opinion ↔ To sway public opinion\nhow humans continue to alter the planet ↔ the impact of human activities on Earth\n把 to influence public opinion 改成 To sway public opinion 再叠加上文 \"how\nhumans alter the planet\" 的名词化转述，正好等价于 To sway public opinion of the\nimpact of human activities on Earth.。\nB) 张冠李戴\nTo more accurately describe the consequences of human activities. 这是\nScientists shaped the history of the terms while attempting to accurately\ndescribe how humans continue to alter the planet. 里科学家的目的，被错配到政客\n头上。\nC) 无中生有\nTo win more popular votes in their campaign activities. 文章只字未提选举或拉票；\nlaunched a campaign 中的 campaign 指\"运动\"而非\"竞选活动\"。\nD) 与原文相反\nTo assure the public of the safety of existing industries. 与原文相反——launched\na campaign to sow doubt 表明政客是制造怀疑、并非给公众吃定心丸。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "As used in a National Academy of Sciences report, the term \"climate change\" differs from \"global warming\" in that_________.", "options": ["A) It sounds less vague", "B) It looks more scientific", "C) It covers more phenomena", "D) It is much closer to reality"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】As used in a National Academy of Sciences report, the term \"climate change\" differs\nfrom \"global warming\" in that_________. 问的是该报告里两词的具体差异，属事实层\n提问。\n【拆句】题干 As used in a National Academy of Sciences report, the term \"climate change\"\ndiffers from \"global warming\" in that_________. 是个待补全的陈述句，主干 \"the term\nclimate change differs from global warming in that ...\"，As 引导的状语限定范围；空格\n由 in that 引导的从句填，选项即从句内容。\n【定位】In 1979, a National Academy of Sciences report used the term \"global warming\" to define\nincreases in the Earth's average surface temperature, while \"climate change\" more broadly\nreferred to the numerous effects of this increase, such as sea-level rise and ocean\nacidification(酸化).\n1979 年，美国国家科学院的一份报告用 global warming 这个术语来指地球地表平均温度\n的上升，而 climate change 则更广泛地指这一升温带来的诸多影响，例如海平面上升和海\n洋酸化。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nit sounds less vague 谈的是\"听起来更不含糊\"，可这个报告本身比较的是两术语的指代\n范围，不是听感；听感是 P5 里 Luntz 的话题，与本题报告无关。\nB) 无中生有\nit looks more scientific 谈\"显得更科学\"，原文从未比较两术语的科学性观感，纯属\n编造。\nC) ✓ 正确\nmore broadly referred to the numerous effects ↔ it covers more phenomena\n预判已经定调\"climate change 范围更宽\"，四个选项里只有 it covers more\nphenomena 直接讲范围差异，与定位句中 more broadly + numerous effects 一一\n对位。\nD) 无中生有\nit is much closer to reality 说\"更贴近现实\"，原文并未在该报告中比较两术语谁更接近\n实际，是无中生有。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What did industrialists of the late 20th century resort to in order to mislead Americans?", "options": ["A) Made-up survey results.", "B) False research findings.", "C) Hired climate experts.", "D) Deliberate choice of words."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What did industrialists of the late 20th century resort to in order to mislead\nAmericans? 问的是实业家采取的具体手段，属对文中明示信息的事实性提问。\n【拆句】题干 What did industrialists of the late 20th century resort to in order to mislead\nAmericans? 主干 \"What did industrialists resort to\"，of the late 20th century 修饰\nindustrialists，in order to mislead Americans 是目的状语；resort to 意为\"诉诸于\"，是\n高频四级词组。\n【定位】Word use played a critical role in developing that doubt. For example, the language and polls\nexpert Frank Luntz wrote a memo encouraging the use of \"climate change\" because the\nphrase sounded less scary than \"global warming,\" reported the Guardian.\n用词起到了关键作用，造就了那种怀疑。例如，语言与民调专家 Frank Luntz 写了一份备忘\n录，建议使用 climate change，因为它听起来不像 global warming 那么吓人——《卫\n报》报道。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nMade-up survey results. 说\"伪造调查结果\"，原文只字未提调查造假，纯属编造。\nB) 无中生有\nFalse research findings. 说\"虚假的研究发现\"，原文从未提及实业家发布假研究，只提\n了使用词汇上的策略。\nC) 张冠李戴\nHired climate experts. 把 the language and polls expert Frank Luntz 的身份错置\n——Luntz 是语言与民调专家，并非气候专家，且原文未说他被实业家雇佣。\nD) ✓ 正确\nWord use played a critical role in developing that doubt. ↔ Deliberate choice of words.\n预判已明确\"在用词上做手脚\"，四个选项里只有 Deliberate choice of words. 谈选词，\n其他三个分别引到调查、研究、专家，都不是文中点出的手段。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "Why did NASA choose the term \"climate change\"?", "options": ["A) To obtain more funds.", "B) For greater precision.", "C) For political needs.", "D) To avoid debate."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why did NASA choose the term \"climate change\"? 是 Why 提问，问 NASA 选这个术\n语的原因，原文必有直接对应句。\n【拆句】题干 Why did NASA choose the term \"climate change\"? 是简单疑问句，主干 \"Why\ndid NASA choose the term\"，引号短语作 choose 的宾语，句式无陷阱。\n【定位】NASA used the term \"climate change\" because it more accurately reflects the wide range of\nchanges to the planet caused by increasing amounts of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.\n美国国家航空航天局之所以使用 climate change 这个术语，是因为它能更准确地反映由大\n气中温室气体增加所引发的地球范围广泛的变化。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nTo obtain more funds. 谈\"获取更多资金\"，原文从未提及 NASA 的预算或经费问题。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmore accurately reflects ↔ For greater precision\n预判已经给出\"为了更准确\"这个核心，四个选项里只有 For greater precision 谈精准\n度，其他三个引到资金、政治、避免争论，都没在 P7 中出现过。\nC) 张冠李戴\nFor political needs. 谈\"出于政治需要\"，这是 political strategists 与 industrialists\nand politicians 的动机，而非 NASA 的；NASA 的选词理由是科学准确性。\nD) 无中生有\nTo avoid debate. 说\"为了避免争论\"，原文 P7 只字未提 NASA 想回避争论；P8 反而表\n明争论一直存在、不可回避。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What is the author's final conclusion?", "options": ["A) Global warming is the more accurate term.", "B) Accuracy of terminology matters in science.", "C) Human activities have serious effects on Earth.", "D) Politics interferes with serious scientific debate."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题）\n【判型】What is the author's final conclusion? 问的是作者的最终结论，是对全文中心的提问，不\n靠某一句的细节解决。\n【拆句】题干 What is the author's final conclusion? 是 What is 句型，主干极简，author's final\n是 conclusion 的双层修饰，提示找全文末尾的总结表态。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nGlobal warming is the more accurate term. 说 global warming 更准确，恰好和\nNASA used the term \"climate change\" because it more accurately reflects the\nwide range of changes 与 whatever you call it 的立场相反。\nB) 以偏概全\nAccuracy of terminology matters in science. 把\"术语精确度\"当作全文主旨，但这只\n是 P3、P7 这两个局部所谈，作者在末段已明确越过术语之争，落点在\"人类影响\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nhuman behavior is warming the planet, with grave consequences ahead ↔\nHuman activities have serious effects on Earth.\nHuman activities have serious effects on Earth. 既概括了 P8 末句的核心判断，也\n覆盖前文 P3 提到的海平面上升、海洋酸化等具体后果，是唯一兼顾主线与全文铺垫的\n选项。\nD) 以偏概全\nPolitics interferes with serious scientific debate. 说\"政治干预严肃科学辩论\"，这只是\nP4\\~P5 局部话题，且原文未给出\"干预了严肃科学辩论\"这样的明确价值判断，更不是作\n者最终结论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2020-12-1"});
